  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 257 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nModel guidance shows consistency in a ridge being over the forecast \nregion Friday morning, as troughing begins to enter the Western \nUnited States. A shortwave trough at 850-mb, as seen in GEFS \nmean-spread guidance, supports winds out of the south to \nsoutheast throughout the day. NBM guidance suggests these winds \nwill be 10-15 MPH and gusting 20-25 MPH, though an NBM \noverestimation bias would suggest winds on the lower ends of \nthese ranges. High temperatures are currently forecast in the \nlower 50s to mid 60s, with the lowest temperatures across \neastern areas (along and east of U.S. Highway 83). However, \nthere is a large spread in 75th and 25th percentile high \ntemperatures from the NBM of 10-15 degrees across most of the \nforecast area. Temperatures in the cooler zone may be allowed to\nreach into the 60s depending on how much solar heating takes \nplace. LREF guidance currently suggests around a 50-70% chance \nthat cloud cover is below 10% in this area, which could provide \nthe warmer conditions. No hazards are expected Friday, though \novernight lows may drop to around freezing. \n\nEnsemble guidance continues to show upper ridging in place Saturday, \nthough troughing continues to approach the area. Southerly flow will \nstill likely be in place, with similar wind speeds to Friday. \nForecast highs are in the mid 50s to lower 60s again, though LREF \nguidance shows only a 10-20% chance for cloud cover below 10%. This \nwould suggest temperatures slightly cooler than Friday, but still at \nor above average (average high is 55). \n\nThe main system to monitor in the long term is the approaching \ntrough set to come through between Saturday night and Sunday. LREF \nclusters, particularly the GEFS and ENS members, have increasingly \nshown this feature as a modestly deep shortwave. Investigating \nfurther, roughly the bottom 25% of members from both the GEFS and \nENS show this deeper trough, alongside a surface low that would \nreach around 1000 mb or lower. These solutions are linked to high \ntemperatures mainly below 50 on Sunday, despite the current forecast \nof upper 50s and lower 60s. Additionally, these solutions show \nrainfall around a few tenths of an inch between Saturday night\nand Sunday. However, the most extreme members (bottom 5%) are \nthe ones that can provide the higher impacts, particularly \nbecause they show high temperatures around freezing alongside \nsome wintry precipitation. The most reasonable scenario, with \nabout 75% confidence, is continued average or above temperatures\nwith perhaps some scattered light precipitation, though it's \nstill something to keep an eye on in case the extreme scenario \noccurs. Confidence in a case with wintry precipitation is 5% or \nless. \n\nOnce this shortwave feature passes, ensembles show ridging returning \nto the area. Forecast highs continue to remain in the upper 50s and \nlower 60s Monday and Tuesday, though this is up to question, as high \ntemperatures will depend on how the Saturday night and Sunday \nscenario plays out. Additionally, guidance shows another storm \nsystem beginning to enter the area at the end of the forecast \nperiod. It is currently unknown what this system will be \ncapable of at this time, as the atmosphere will undergo \npotentially significant modification before then.",
